    {
      "name": "edit_video",
      "description": "Create a local video with sequential v1 clips and nonoverlapping v2 clips above them. Every clip requires track; v2 requires start seconds, v1 forbids start. Crop uses source-edge percentages; place selects full, halves, corner picture-in-picture or a custom normalized box. Omitted crop/place mean zero crop/full. Supports trims (at least 3 frames (0.1 seconds)), fill/fit, pan (0–1), zoom (1–3), photos (0.1–30 seconds), audio and source-second punch-ins. Up to 300 clips, maximum end 180 seconds. after_clip indexes the request array and must target v1; the incoming neighbor is the next v1, skipping v2. Transitions clamp to adjacent clip limits. Captions and pause detection use v1 only, even when muted. Removing pauses ripples both tracks and overlays; conflicting v1 transitions return an explicit error. Align sources manually; no automatic sync or drift correction. Example: put reference.mp4 on top muted, me.mp4 on the bottom, crop 5% off the sides, start together, Clean captions from my voice. Captions are made on your computer in English; optional corrections replace exact words/phrases. Specify caption style clean/boxed/minimal/karaoke/bold-keyword/word-pop/highlight-box/neon/pop-up/typewriter/outline/editorial/gradient/mixed, position upper/center/lower and size s/m/l. Style mixed gives every word its own font, weight, color and spacing; add captions.mix with a preset (creator or editorial), seed and palette, and captions.word_styles to restyle chosen words by source seconds (font, weight, italic, fill, tracking, leading, underline, highlight). Example: mixed-font Creator trio captions with a cream, yellow and pink palette, underline the words spoken from 2.0 to 2.8 seconds in the first clip. Titles: texts adds up to 100 native titles of up to 120 characters, each either bound to a v1 clip by clip (shown for that clip's whole span; optional timestamp source = the clip's source in-point as mm:ss, or timeline) or placed with start and duration timeline seconds. Defaults: sans 800, size m, centered, no backing; set font, weight, italic, fill, size, x, y and backing to restyle. Titles are free and never leave your computer. Rapid intro: rapid_intro takes 5–7 v1 clip indices in teaser order and one label per clip; it prepends a 3–6-frame cut (frames_per_clip, default 5) of each clip's trim, muted by default, then plays the unchanged v1 body with each clip's label and timestamp (source in-point mm:ss by default; timeline or none). Everything with an absolute time (v2 starts, overlay starts, explicit titles) moves later by the intro length, so give those times as for the edit without the intro. Build it in one call. This tool never looks at pictures: use the descriptions the user gives or ask for them, and never invent them from file names. Example: “Make a five-frame rapid intro from grind.mp4, pour.mp4, stir.mp4, steam.mp4 and serve.mp4, then replay them with source timestamps and the labels Grind beans, Pour water, Stir, Steam milk and Serve, in 9:16.” is one call with five v1 clips, rapid_intro {clips:[0,1,2,3,4], frames_per_clip:5, timestamp:\"source\", labels:[{clip:0,text:\"Grind beans\"},…]} and aspect 9x16. No overlays or unchanged free overlays with Clean/Boxed/Minimal captions and no word_styles export without login; titles and rapid intro are free; mixed captions, word styles and other edits need Pro. 1080p default, 720p also free. Media never uploads; Studio exports never enter account history. Returns job_id; poll job_status.",
